WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.272
Integer safety in Rust is a repeatable
engineering practice.

2
00:00:03.352 --> 00:00:07.191
Start by choosing integer types based on
meaning, range, and boundary requirements.

3
00:00:07.271 --> 00:00:09.083
Signedness, width, platform behavior,

4
00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:11.456
and serialization needs should all be
deliberate.

5
00:00:11.536 --> 00:00:15.001
A number's type should help reviewers
understand what the value represents.

6
00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:17.640
Validate external numbers after parsing
and before use.

7
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.066
Parsing confirms representation, not
permission.

8
00:00:20.146 --> 00:00:23.398
Check range, unit, tenant context,
operation context, related fields,

9
00:00:23.478 --> 00:00:24.491
and default behavior.

10
00:00:24.571 --> 00:00:28.303
Reject unsupported values clearly and
convert validated values into domain types

11
00:00:28.383 --> 00:00:29.076
when possible.

12
00:00:29.156 --> 00:00:32.141
Use fallible conversions when narrowing or
changing signedness.

13
00:00:32.221 --> 00:00:33.767
Treat `as` casts as review points.

14
00:00:33.847 --> 00:00:37.312
Choose arithmetic behavior deliberately:
checked, saturating, wrapping,

15
00:00:37.392 --> 00:00:39.578
or overflowing only when the domain calls
for it.

16
00:00:39.658 --> 00:00:42.271
Test numeric edge cases around every
important behavior.

17
00:00:42.351 --> 00:00:44.483
Treat indexes, offsets, lengths,
capacities,

18
00:00:44.563 --> 00:00:47.229
and allocation calculations as high-value
review points.

19
00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:49.441
Use checked arithmetic for size
calculations.

20
00:00:49.521 --> 00:00:50.694
Apply explicit maximums.

21
00:00:50.774 --> 00:00:53.173
Fail safely when a request exceeds
supported limits.

22
00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:56.399
Resource exhaustion is still a security
concern in memory-safe code.

23
00:00:56.479 --> 00:00:59.890
Finally, document numeric contracts at
protocol, serialization, unsafe,

24
00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:00.877
and FFI boundaries.

25
00:01:00.957 --> 00:01:04.156
Translate external representations into
validated Rust types early.

26
00:01:04.236 --> 00:01:07.648
Maintain tests for minimums, maximums,
invalid values, conversion edges,

27
00:01:07.728 --> 00:01:09.860
and boundary combinations as the code
evolves.
